invariant: INV-17
file: sha256_hw.c
catches: test/docker-aes-runtime-qemu.sh sha2-equiv
reason: the x86-64 rounds keep four schedule words plus their constants in a
  local of their own, which the compiler must hold in memory, where they
  belong in the block_state. compress_blocks wipes the block_state alone,
  so its dead frame keeps the last four sums of the last block. The
  constants are public, so the sums give the schedule words, and under
  HMAC the first block's schedule is the key's. Every digest is still
  right. bin/sha2_equiv_test copies the stack below each kind of call and
  looks for four words in a row that the call computed from its input,
  each schedule word plus its constant among them. The edit is in the
  x86-64 arm, so the catch is the script, which builds the binary for
  x86-64 and runs it under qemu-x86_64. The lane skips without docker, and
  this then reports unguarded rather than caught.

--- old
    c->with_constants = _mm_add_epi32(current, load_words(&K[4 * group]));
    c->cdgh = _mm_sha256rnds2_epu32(c->cdgh, c->abef, c->with_constants);
    c->abef = _mm_sha256rnds2_epu32(c->abef, c->cdgh, _mm_shuffle_epi32(c->with_constants, 0x0e));
--- new
    volatile words with_constants = _mm_add_epi32(current, load_words(&K[4 * group]));
    c->cdgh = _mm_sha256rnds2_epu32(c->cdgh, c->abef, with_constants);
    c->abef = _mm_sha256rnds2_epu32(c->abef, c->cdgh, _mm_shuffle_epi32(with_constants, 0x0e));
